WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.604 line:0%
Gav:<i> We're trying to film
 something that is quite rare,</i>

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:07.306 line:0%
<i> but incredibly dangerous
 to a firefighter,</i>

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:08.741 line:0%
<i> and it's called a backdraft.</i>

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:11.544 align:start size:84% position:16% line:8%
- Man: Open it up.
- Backdraft initiated.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.048
Dan:
<i> Oh, it's like a dragon.</i>

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.683
- Whoa!
- Whoa!

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:20.320
God!

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:32.198 align:start position:0% line:0%
Probably one of the most
terrifying videos
that I've shot.

00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:34.367 align:start position:0% line:0%
'Cause it leads you
to imagine,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.835
you know, what could
happen in real life.

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:38.337
Just to see smoke coming
out of there one second.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.640
The next second,
there's huge explosion.

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.341
We once again put the Phantom

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:44.143
in an incredibly
dangerous position.

00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:46.112
Whoa!

00:00:46.145 --> 00:00:47.713
( both sigh )

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:49.348 align:start size:50% position:50%
We should really
stop doing that.

00:00:49.381 --> 00:00:51.184 size:88% position:44%
But once again, it survived.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.819
Dan:<i> We did have
 some marshmallows</i>

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.587
that seemed to be a bit cooked,
but the Phantom was okay.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.389
One of the most
interesting shots, I thought.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:58.825
<i> was when all the marshmallows,
 they all got knocked</i>

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.593
<i> just from the force
 of the explosion.</i>

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:02.128
<i> So it could easily blow you
 off your feet.</i>

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:04.197
Dan:
<i> It was, like, surprising.</i>

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.199 align:start position:0%
I feel like a lot of the time
when we're doing
an intro to a video,

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:08.367 align:start position:0%
we're stood somewhere
quite nice and comfortable

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.369 align:start position:0%
and then we go on
to do the experiment.

00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:13.106 align:start position:0%
That was the most stressful
intro I've ever been a part of.

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
Not gonna lie, in the intro
I was trying to be all
casual and cool.

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:17.510 align:start size:78% position:22%
Like, "Yeah, yeah, yeah."

00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:19.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, trying to make a gag
about how it was cold outside.

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:22.515 align:start size:91% position:9%
I was like,
"I've gotta get out of here."
It was just so hot.

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:24.450 align:start size:72% position:28%
Quite cold outside,
so I'm going to warm
myself up a little bit.

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:26.385 align:start position:0%
I think
I'm warm enough, actually.

00:01:26.418 --> 00:01:29.322 align:start size:84% position:16%
- All right.
  Oh, it's pretty hot!
- Let's head outside.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.657
I came out,
and I was just like,
"Holy cow."

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.160
Yeah, I had the cameras roll
right after we got of there

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.761
so we could talk about
what it was like

00:01:35.794 --> 00:01:39.298
to be inside a burning room
with a rollover on the ceiling.

00:01:39.331 --> 00:01:44.403 size:78% position:39%
Okay, so we just finished
the intro to part one.

00:01:44.436 --> 00:01:46.272 align:start size:72% position:28%
I mean, if you're gonna
learn to do an intro

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:48.774 align:start size:78% position:22%
in one take or two takes,
I feel like

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:51.244 align:start position:0%
-<i> that's the best way to do it. </i>
-<i> We knelt down between takes</i>

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.845
<i> just 'cause it was
 getting so hot</i>

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:54.680
<i> and stood back up
 to do the second take.</i>

00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:57.250
<i> But obviously you sort of
 retain a lot of the heat</i>

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.252 align:start size:84% position:16%
-<i> that you had
 in the first take. </i>
-<i> Yeah.</i>

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.786 align:start position:0%
And the end
of the second take,

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:03.456 align:start position:0%
I felt like
the last ten seconds of that
just got so hot.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.424
<i> The air I was breathing
 was hot.</i>

00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:07.927
<i> My shoulder--
 my left shoulder boiling.</i>

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.629
Dan:<i> And my shoulder
 is still warm.</i>

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.430
<i> Like, it's still hot.</i>

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:13.499 align:start size:81% position:19%
I felt your shoulder here.
This part is still warm.

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:17.436 align:start size:75% position:25%
My actual like skin
through all those layers
is still, like, hot.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:18.905 align:start position:0%
That was an experience.

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:21.174
All right, here's one
of our wide shots.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:24.443
It sort of had
the boomiest wide that we had.

00:02:24.476 --> 00:02:28.381
Um, and using your very
technical marshmallow sticks,

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.283 size:84% position:58%
we can figure out the speed

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:31.784 align:start size:53% position:47%
of this backdraft
at its fastest.

00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.687
<i> Yeah, so I think
 I'll start timing</i>

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.890 align:start size:94% position:6%
- past this one to this one.
- Marshmallow one.

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:39.325
Gav:
<i> Okay, that many.</i>

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:44.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Dan:<i> To there. </i>
-<i> Let's stop it there.</i>

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.832
So at our shooting
frame rate of 1,000,

00:02:46.865 --> 00:02:50.203
<i> it went from here to here
 in 145 frames.</i>

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.205
<i> That's five feet.</i>

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:55.808
<i> The speed was 35 feet
 per second.</i>

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:57.944 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- So that's 23 miles an hour.
- Yeah.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:00.479 align:start position:0% line:0%
Which is sort of
Olympic sprinter speeds.

00:03:00.512 --> 00:03:02.415 align:start position:0% line:0%
You have to be literally
an Olympic sprinter.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:03.482 align:start position:0% line:0%
It would be
a real dramatic scene

00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:04.784 align:start position:0% line:0%
running in front
of a backdraft.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.553
You'd have to already be
at full tilt

00:03:07.586 --> 00:03:10.256
going that way at this point
to not get hit by that.

00:03:10.289 --> 00:03:13.526
<i> And then it sort of slows down
 as it gets further along.</i>

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:15.228
<i> I'd say
 that's the fastest point</i>

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:16.896
<i> that we can measure on this.</i>

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:19.265
Dan:<i> It's really busting out
 of there, though, still.</i>

00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:20.633 size:88% position:44%
So you've seen it in action,

00:03:20.666 --> 00:03:22.535 align:start position:0%
but to find out more
about what causes a backdraft,

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:24.470 size:84% position:42%
we spoke to Fire Chief Ken,

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:25.671 align:start position:0%
who's... there.

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:26.939
There he is, yeah.

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:29.508
We're here
with Chief Ken Bailey

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:30.576 line:0%
from Travis County
Fire Rescue.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:32.378 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Good to see you guys.
- Hello.

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:33.646 line:0%
- That was a good handshake.
- That's a solid handshake.

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.715 line:0%
That is a solid handshake, yeah.

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.918
So, what exactly is a backdraft
and how does it work?

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:40.686
So, a backdraft is where

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:43.356
<i> all the contents
 are started on fire,</i>

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.358
<i> and then all the oxygen
 is consumed</i>

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:46.592
<i> in a sealed container.</i>

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:47.627
<i> What ends up happening</i>

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.629
<i> is we reintroduce oxygen</i>

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.864
<i> and we have a rapid
 explosion of the gases</i>

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:54.533
<i> which were the products
 of combustion.</i>

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:57.637 align:start position:0%
And how often
would that happen
in normal firefighting?

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.506 size:84% position:58%
You know, most people go
a career without seeing it.

00:04:00.539 --> 00:04:02.341 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So it's a very rare
  occurrence.
- Right.

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:05.044 align:start size:81% position:19%
- But a very sudden
  and dangerous one.
- Exactly.

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:06.812
I heard somewhere
that you have less time

00:04:06.845 --> 00:04:08.614
to get out
of a house fire now.

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:11.951 align:start size:91% position:9%
So, back when our parents
had furnishings in the house,

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:15.588 align:start size:63% position:38%
it was probably made
of cotton or wool,

00:04:15.621 --> 00:04:17.657 align:start size:78% position:22%
and those will only
let off so much heat over
a certain period of time.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.026 align:start size:75% position:25%
The heat release rate
on those were different.

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:22.561 align:start size:59% position:41%
And the products
that we buy today,

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:23.996 align:start size:56% position:44%
they're cheaper,
they're Styrofoam.

00:04:24.029 --> 00:04:25.898 align:start size:78% position:22%
Look at it as solid fuel.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:27.733
<i> And so the heat release rate</i>

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:30.403
<i> is much, much faster on that.</i>

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.371
<i> It very quickly
 consumes the oxygen.</i>

00:04:32.404 --> 00:04:33.873
<i> If the home was sealed,</i>

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:35.675 align:start size:91% position:9%
and then
the fire department shows up,

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:37.410 align:start size:59% position:41%
they open the door,
and we have that

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:40.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
introduction of oxygen
in that sealed compartment,

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.349 size:84% position:58%
it gives us the challenge
of the potential backdraft.

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:45.618 align:start position:0%
I was just blown away
when we were in there
doing our intro

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:48.321 align:start position:0%
just how hot you still feel
through all the gear.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.323 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Dan: I'm still
  cooling off now.
- Gav: Yeah.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:52.458 align:start size:91% position:9%
It was like half an hour ago.
I'm, like, still warm.

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.593 align:start position:0%
And with, like, a beard,
which I wouldn't have

00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:56.429 align:start size:91% position:9%
- if I was a firefighter.
- Non-regulation.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.531 align:start position:0%
The seal on my mask
was letting in hot air, too.

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:01.600 align:start position:0%
So that was-- I've got--
think I'm gonna shave this off
before we go back in.

00:05:01.633 --> 00:05:03.436 align:start size:75% position:25%
Next time you come back,
you should just shave,

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:04.804 align:start size:53% position:47%
like, a strip
for the gas mask.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:07.773 align:start position:0%
So, say I'm just
a person in the house,

00:05:07.806 --> 00:05:10.076 align:start position:0%
my house has caught fire,
I called the fire department.

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:12.445 align:start position:0%
Is there any state
that I can leave my house

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.613 size:88% position:44%
that's safer from backdrafts

00:05:14.646 --> 00:05:16.449 align:start position:0%
or shall I just get
the hell out of there?

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.517 align:start size:75% position:25%
Getting out of the house
is the most important.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.787 align:start size:75% position:25%
We would much rather
you burn your house down
and save your life

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.458 align:start size:75% position:25%
than try to stay with it
or delay 911 by--

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.793 align:start size:75% position:25%
That's one
of the problems we have,
is people will--

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:30.129 align:start size:59% position:41%
will think,
"Hey, we can handle
this situation."

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:32.698
And then that delays
a call to 911,

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:36.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then when we get there,
it's a much larger fire
than it needed to be.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:38.904 align:start size:75% position:25%
So if you're in a room
with all locked doors
and there's a fire door,

00:05:38.937 --> 00:05:41.073 align:start size:66% position:34%
and there's a window,
and there's a fire,

00:05:41.106 --> 00:05:42.575 align:start position:0%
how should you get out?

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:45.077 align:start size:56% position:44%
How's the best way
to get out safely?

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.480 align:start size:63% position:38%
So if you're in
an interior corridor

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:49.782 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you're trying to make it
through the fire area,

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:51.650 align:start size:78% position:22%
the area that's involved,

00:05:51.683 --> 00:05:53.419 align:start size:81% position:19%
and you have the choice
between that and a window,

00:05:53.452 --> 00:05:55.388 align:start size:66% position:34%
I'd choose the window
before the other.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.857
All it takes
is a couple of breaths
of that carbon monoxide

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.826 align:start size:72% position:28%
and hydrogen cyanide
and some of the other
products of combustion,

00:06:00.859 --> 00:06:03.929 align:start size:63% position:38%
and your chances are
that you won't
make it to the door.

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:05.698 align:start position:0%
We tried to contact
a lot of people

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:07.733 align:start position:0%
to get this simulated

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:09.668 align:start position:0%
and you guys are one
of the very few people

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:12.037 size:78% position:39%
who said that you'd be up
to recreate a backdraft.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.672 size:75% position:38%
And you actually did it,

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:15.708 size:84% position:42%
so we're extremely grateful
that you could help us out

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:17.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
- and make this video with us.
- Yeah, very much, Ken.

00:06:17.609 --> 00:06:19.478
- That was really informative.
- Thank you. It was our honor.

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:20.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Also scary, as well.
- We appreciate it.

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:22.848 align:start size:88% position:13%
All right, back to you,
Gav and Dan.

00:06:22.881 --> 00:06:24.417 align:start position:0%
Thanks, us.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.685 align:start position:0%
Now, very few people
will come into contact

00:06:26.718 --> 00:06:29.188 align:start position:0%
with a real, full-sized
backdraft like that.

00:06:29.221 --> 00:06:32.525 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Thank goodness.
- But there is a much smaller
  version of a backdraft

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.560 align:start position:0%
that people might be
familiar with.

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:35.728 align:start position:0%
So why don't we go outside
and do an experiment?

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:37.496
- All right.
- All right.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:40.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, Dan, I'm really glad
we film this in winter.

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:41.634 align:start position:0%
Yeah, it's nice, isn't it?
Good barbecue weather.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:42.968 align:start size:72% position:28%
Nice time for a barbie.

00:06:43.001 --> 00:06:44.970 align:start position:0%
- Yeah.
- So, using what we've learned,

00:06:45.003 --> 00:06:47.139 size:84% position:58%
you think you can create
a mini backdraft with this?

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:48.474
- I think so.
- All right.

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:49.742 align:start position:0% line:0%
I'll set the scene.
It's the summer.

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:51.710 align:start position:0% line:0%
It's not raining.
Got a nice, hot barbecue.

00:06:51.743 --> 00:06:53.479 align:start position:0% line:0%
It's been on for awhile.
Hot coals.

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:54.480 align:start position:0% line:0%
And we got our chicken.

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:55.781
It's gonna be covered in oil,

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:57.783 align:start position:0%
'cause that's how
you cook chicken, isn't it?

00:06:57.816 --> 00:06:59.485
If I just introduce the fuel--

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:01.720 align:start position:0%
so this is like the wood
in the back of the container.

00:07:01.753 --> 00:07:04.957 align:start position:0%
I'll just pop that
on the thing.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.159 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Oh, you can tell
  it's already hot.
- Yeah, it's already hot.

00:07:07.192 --> 00:07:09.895 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Immediately lit up.
- Then I close this down,
  shut the flames down,

00:07:09.928 --> 00:07:13.199 align:start position:0%
and then when
I reintroduce the oxygen...

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:14.900 align:start position:0%
it should pop out.

00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:16.068
- Very nice.
- See that?

00:07:16.101 --> 00:07:17.437 align:start size:47% position:53%
That was great.

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:21.740 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- Dan:<i> Just medium chicken. </i>
-<i> Medium chicken.</i>

00:07:21.773 --> 00:07:23.909 line:0%
Dan:<i> Oh, you see all the smoke
 going in? Do you see that?</i>

00:07:23.942 --> 00:07:25.578 line:0%
- Gav:<i> You see it rush out. </i>
-<i> And that's that same pattern.</i>

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.213
Gav:<i> It's a similar effect.</i>

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:29.548
<i> The smoke's kind of getting
 sucked back around.</i>

00:07:29.581 --> 00:07:32.585
Dan:<i> Yeah. That oxygen just
 rushes in and then boom.</i>

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:34.987
 Gav:<i> And then it just ignites
 all the-- the smoke, really.</i>

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:36.755 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Dan:<i> Yeah. </i>
-<i> A little mushroom cloud</i>

00:07:36.788 --> 00:07:38.591
<i> for a second there.
 Look at that.</i>

